.Dd September 19, 2026
.Dt RANDOM 4
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm random ,
.Nm urandom
.Nd entropy devices
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa /dev/random
.Pp
.Pa /dev/urandom
.Sh DESCRIPTION
Both devices read from the kernel's single ChaCha20 random byte stream,
the same source used by
.Xr getrandom 2 .
Neither device blocks waiting for entropy.
.Pa /dev/random
is not a blocking entropy pool as on Linux.
.Pp
The generator is seeded at boot.
The kernel first tries RDSEED, then RDRAND.
A complete seed from either instruction is trusted.
If neither supplies a complete seed, the kernel uses the timestamp
counter, CMOS seconds, and the PIT tick.
This fallback is untrusted; reads from either device then fail with
.Er EIO .
.Pp
The boot record identifies the source:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
entropy: ready: RDSEED, trusted
entropy: ready: RDRAND, trusted
entropy: ready: jitter, best-effort; the crypto RNG fails closed
.Ed
.Sh FILES
Both devices have mode 0666.
.Bl -tag -width /dev/urandom
.It Pa /dev/random
Major 1, minor 8.
.It Pa /dev/urandom
Major 1, minor 9.
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr getrandom 2 ,
.Xr null 4
